---
title: Progressive Rollout Lifecycle
config:
    layout: elk
---
stateDiagram-v2
    direction TB

    [*] --> soaking: from process release (first stage activated)

    soaking --> ready_for_stage_analysis: soak window elapses (time)

    ready_for_stage_analysis --> analyzing: on-call claims stage analysis

    analyzing --> soaking: on-call advances to next stage
    analyzing --> complete: on-call confirms final stage healthy
    analyzing --> holding: on-call pauses rollout
    analyzing --> killing: on-call claims to kill rollout

    holding --> analyzing: on-call re-claims held rollout

    killing --> kill_switched: on-call disables flag, files compensating bug

    complete --> [*]: terminal (shipped) — release lifecycle resumes at monitoring
    kill_switched --> [*]: terminal (reverted) — release lifecycle transitions to rolling_back

    note right of soaking
        Resting. Flag is at the current stage percentage (e.g., 1%, 10%, 50%). No agent active; the system is gathering data during the soak window.
    end note

    note right of analyzing
        Working. On-call reviews metrics for the current stage: error rates, latency, business KPIs. Outcomes: advance, complete, hold, or kill.
    end note

    note right of soaking
        Advancing to the next stage (say, 10% → 50%) moves back to soaking with the new percentage active. Stage progression is: 1% → 10% → 50% → 100% by default, configurable per release.
    end note

    note right of holding
        Resting. On-call paused due to ambiguous signal; no advance, no kill. Used when metrics are concerning but not clearly bad. Typically resolved within hours.
    end note

    note right of killing
        Working. On-call is executing the kill: flag off, compensating bug filed, release lifecycle notified. This is a fast state — usually minutes.
    end note

    note right of complete
        Terminal (shipped). Rollout reached 100% and the final stage soak was clean. Hand back to release lifecycle for final monitoring.
    end note

    note right of kill_switched
        Terminal (reverted). Flag is disabled; no users see the change. The release lifecycle transitions to rolling_back. A compensating bug enters refinement as a new raw issue.
    end note
